# Capability Capsule protocols

## Definition

A **Capability Capsule (CC)** is a versioned, installable package exposing one or more related operations.

| Record | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Declaration | What each operation does, requires, changes and returns. |
| Release | The immutable declaration and executable bindings. |
| Call | One operation bound to inputs, authority and execution limits. |
| Receipt | The observed result, effects, checks and output references. |

## System connections

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    author["Tool author"] --> declaration["CC declaration"]
    declaration --> factory["Software Factory"]
    factory --> release["Immutable CC release"]
    factory --> build["Build provenance"]
    release --> qualifier["Independent qualification"]
    qualifier --> catalog["CC catalog"]
    catalog -->|operation discovery| agent["Lab agent"]
    agent -->|request and inputs| admission["Admission and call binding"]
    release -->|release identity| admission
    admission --> boundCall["Bound call"]
    boundCall --> runner["Runner"]
    runner --> outputs["Outputs"]
    runner --> receipt["Receipt"]
    receipt -.->|identifies| boundCall
    boundCall -.->|pins| release
    build -.->|identifies build subject| release
    outputs --> evaluation["Independent evaluation"]
    receipt --> evaluation
```

| Strata/Muse role | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Factory | Build the candidate engine and any reusable CCs. A product may contain zero CCs. |
| Profiler or benchmark CC | Measure the selected engine under declared conditions. |
| Lab | Bind the engine, model, workload, environment and measurement tool. |
| Evaluator | Assess the evidence against the frozen comparison policy. |

## Minimal declaration

```yaml
cc: "1"
id: text.trim
version: 1.0.0
carrier: fixture.invalid/text@sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa
does: Remove leading and trailing ASCII spaces. An all-space input returns an empty string.
run: /tools/trim
takes: {text: string}
gives: {trimmed: string}
```

| Caller question | Answer for `text.trim/run` |
|---|---|
| What does it do? | Removes leading and trailing ASCII spaces. |
| What must I supply? | One string named `text`. |
| What can it change? | No external mutation is declared. |
| What will I receive? | One string named `trimmed`. An empty string is valid. |
| What can fail? | Wrong input type fails validation. A process crash fails the call. |
| What is checked? | The runner checks the declared output type. |

